Ask the app who owns a connection on Android, and ask it about the right one.

Routes for specific apps are "process" rules, and on Android the process name
is the package that opened the connection. xray has a hook for that
(common/net/find_process_android.go), but nothing registers it in a plain
build, so every lookup fails. The app owns the VPN and is the only caller the
system answers through ConnectivityManager.getConnectionOwnerUid. It listens
on an abstract unix socket and passes its name in KEQDROID_OWNER_SOCKET (see
forkexec.c and ConnectionOwnerServer.kt); main/owner_android.go registers a
finder that asks there. The protocol is the one mihomo uses
(mihomo-android-uid-owner.patch): one line "network srcip srcport dstip
dstport", one line back with the package name, empty when unknown.

The router asks again for every rule with a process condition, so answers are
kept for two seconds.

app/router/condition.go: the system finds a TCP connection only by both ends,
and the matcher passed the target, which sniffing (destOverride without
routeOnly, and fakedns always) has already turned into a domain. The lookup
then went out with no destination and failed for every site xray sniffed. The
matcher now passes the address the connection was opened to,
Outbound.OriginalTarget, the same field app/router/webhook.go reads.

Built by tool/build_xray_cores.ps1. Checked against xray-core v26.10.10:
neither file changed upstream.

